Here is an example of a Java program that demonstrates exception handling:

```java
public class ExceptionExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Code that might throw an exception
            int num = 10;
            int div = 0;
            int ans = num / div;
            System.out.println("The answer is " + ans);
        } catch (ArithmeticException ae) {
            // Code to handle ArithmeticException
            System.out.println("Arithmetic Exception: Cannot divide by zero");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Code to handle any other Exception
            System.out.println("An error occurred: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            // Code that will always execute, regardless of whether an exception was thrown
            System.out.println("Exception handling complete");
        }
    }
}
```

In the above program, the code within the `try` block is monitored for exceptions. If any exception occurs, it is caught by the corresponding `catch` block. In this case, dividing by zero triggers an `ArithmeticException` which is caught and handled by the first `catch` block, which prints out a message indicating that the error occurred. The second `catch` block is there to catch any other types of exceptions that may occur. Finally, the `finally` block contains code that will always be executed, regardless of whether or not an exception was thrown. In this case, it prints out a message indicating that exception handling is complete.

Here is a Java program that can be used to print all permutations of an array:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Permutations {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {1, 2, 3};
        permutate(array, array.length);

    }
    private static void permutate(int[] array, int n) {
        if (n == 1) {
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(array));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            swap(array, i, n - 1);
            permutate(array, n - 1);
            swap(array, i, n - 1);
        }

    }
    private static void swap(int[] array, int i, int j) {
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
}
```

This program uses recursion to print all permutations of a given array. The main method calls the permutate method, which takes the array and its length as arguments. The permutate method swaps elements of the array and recursively calls itself until all permutations have been printed. Then the swap method is used to swap the elements back to their original positions.
